● Z-Matrix Format Explanation

  A z-matrix (or internal coordinate matrix) is a way to represent molecular geometry using internal coordinates (bonds, angles, dihedrals) rather than Cartesian
  coordinates. Each line defines one atom's position relative to previously defined atoms.

  Format Structure:

  ATOM  CONN1  CONN2  CONN3    DISTANCE    ANGLE      DIHEDRAL

  Column Breakdown:

  1. ATOM - Element symbol (N, H, C, O)
  2. CONN1 - Bond connection (atom number, 1-based)
  3. CONN2 - Angle connection (atom number, 1-based)
  4. CONN3 - Dihedral connection (atom number, 1-based)
  5. DISTANCE - Bond length in Angstroms
  6. ANGLE - Bond angle in degrees
  7. DIHEDRAL - Dihedral angle in degrees

  How it Works:

  Line 1: N  0  0  0  0.000000  0.00000  0.00000
  - First atom (N) is placed at origin
  - No connections (all 0s), no internal coordinates needed

  Line 2: H  1  0  0  1.144956  0.00000  0.00000
  - H atom connected to atom 1 (N)
  - Distance: 1.145 Å from N
  - No angle/dihedral (only 1 previous atom)

  Line 3: H  1  2  0  0.967439  119.715  0.00000
  - H atom connected to atom 1 (N)
  - Distance: 0.967 Å from N
  - Angle: 119.7° with respect to N-H₂ bond
  - No dihedral (only 2 previous atoms)

  Line 4: H  1  2  3  0.999877  97.144  127.095
  - H atom connected to atom 1 (N)
  - Distance: 1.000 Å from N
  - Angle: 97.1° with respect to N-H₂ bond
  - Dihedral: 127.1° with respect to H₃-N-H₂-H₃ plane

  Key Points:

  - Sequential Definition: Each atom is defined using only previously defined atoms
  - 1-based Indexing: Atom numbers start from 1 (not 0)
  - Internal Coordinates: Uses chemically meaningful distances, angles, and dihedrals
  - Redundancy: The same z-matrix can represent the same molecule with different internal coordinates chosen

  This format is particularly useful for:
  - Conformational analysis (tracking how bonds rotate)
  - Geometry optimization (easier to constrain internal coordinates)
  - Chemical intuition (distances and angles have direct physical meaning)

  This script reads this z-matrix and calculates these same internal coordinates for each frame of the trajectory, allowing one to track how the molecular geometry
  changes over time.
